  "why": "The statement asks whether T-free graphs are χ-bounded for every tree T, so settling it for a family of trees (paths, radius-two trees, the Kierstead–Zhu family, distant stars, mixed multibrooms, subdivided stars via Scott) is a case; all of these predate the snapshot. Every post-snapshot event changes the question instead: Davies–Yuditsky restricts the host graphs to bounded boxicity (different hypotheses, not a restriction to a subset of trees); Nguyen–Scott–Seymour bound stable sets and fractional χ rather than χ; Reed–Yuditsky is about almost all graphs; Nguyen's P5 result is the stronger polynomial form for a tree whose χ-boundedness was already known since 1987, so it adds nothing to the question as stated; the rest are signed, domatic, multi-exclusion or induced-subdivision analogues. The closest calls were Davies–Yuditsky (case vs variant) and P5 (case of the polynomial strengthening vs variant). I chose variant for both because neither proves χ-boundedness for any tree not already covered. No AI involvement was found in any event.",
